The punishment number for an integer n is defined as the sum of the squares of all integers i (1 ≤ i ≤ n) such that the decimal representation of i² can be partitioned into one or more contiguous parts that sum up to i.

For example, for n=10:

- i=9, 9²=81, "81" can be partitioned as "8" and "1", 8 + 1 = 9, so 9 contributes 81.
- i=10, 10²=100, "100" can be partitioned as "10" and "0", 10 + 0 = 10, so 10 contributes 100.
- Other i don't satisfy this property.

The punishment number is therefore 81 + 100 = 181.

